course: dictionary
lesson: 14396
title: Outlast: lasting longer than others
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To outlast something means to last longer than it. «The little lighthouse stood through the storm and outlasted it.» The storm stopped first, and the lighthouse was still standing. Look for what stopped first, and the thing that keeps going is the one that outlasts it.
explain: Outlast can also mean to stay in a contest longer than someone else. «Priya was tired, but she outlasted all the other runners.» The others stopped, and Priya kept going. Ask yourself: who was still going at the end?
explain: Outlast often has an object after it, such as outlast the storm or outlast the others. Read the words after it to find out what was beaten or what stopped first. Then swap in your meaning, last longer, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Outlast is a verb: outlast, outlasts, outlasted, outlasting.
fact: «Outlast» means to last longer than something else.
fact: «Outlast» can also mean to stay alive or stay in a contest longer than someone else.
fact: «Outlasted» is the past tense: «The old torch outlasted all the new batteries.»
exercise: «The old torch outlasted all the new batteries.» What does outlasted mean here? | *Lasted longer than the batteries | Was bought at the same time as the batteries | Was broken by the batteries | Ran out before the batteries
exercise: Priya was tired, but she outlasted the others in the race. What does outlasted mean here? | Fell over at the start | Stopped before the others | *Kept going for longer than the others | Shouted at the others
exercise: «The lighthouse outlasted the storm and stood tall in the morning sun.» Find and copy the word that means lasted longer. | sun | storm | *outlasted | morning
test: Which word means nearly the same as outlast? | *Survive | Give up | Break | Begin
test: Which word is the past tense of outlast? | outlasting | outlast | outlasts | *outlasted
exam: Which sentence shows something outlasting something else? | The rain stopped before the picnic began | The bell rang twice at lunch | Nobody wanted to go to the park | *Tom's bike light outlasted his friend's and still shone at midnight
